 1.3 Let $n>0$ and assume the alternative for $n'$ variables, where $n=\sigma(n')=n'\cup\{n'\}$ by [F5] and [[def-natural-numbers]]. For $c\in\mathbb R^n$ define $a\in\mathbb R^{n'}$ by $a(k):=c(k)$ for $k<n'$, and put $\alpha:=c(n')\in\mathbb R$; this separates its first $n'$ coordinates and its last coordinate. Conversely, each $u\in\mathbb R^{n'}$ and $t\in\mathbb R$ determine the unique $b\in\mathbb R^n$ with $b(k)=u(k)$ for $k<n'$ and $b(n')=t$. For every row $c_j$, define $a_j(k):=c_j(k)$ for $k<n'$ and $\alpha_j:=c_j(n')$. Partition the row indices into $P=\{j:\alpha_j>0\}$, $N=\{j:\alpha_j<0\}$, and $Z=\{j:\alpha_j=0\}$. Retain each row $(a_j,h_j)$ for $j\in Z$. For every $p\in P$ and $q\in N$, add the reduced row $((-\alpha_q)a_p+\alpha_p a_q,\;(-\alpha_q)h_p+\alpha_p h_q)$. Every multiplier used is positive, so these row combinations preserve the inequality directions by [F3]. [A1, F1, F3, F5, algebra]
 
-1.4 The reduced system in $u\in\mathbb R^{n'}$ is feasible exactly when the original is, using the coordinate reconstruction in step 1.3. Indeed, a solution $(u,t)$ implies every retained inequality; adding row $p$ multiplied by $-\alpha_q>0$ to row $q$ multiplied by $\alpha_p>0$ cancels $t$ and gives each pair row. Conversely, if $u$ satisfies the reduced system, rows in $P$ impose finitely many lower bounds $t\ge L_p:=(h_p-a_p\cdot u)/\alpha_p$, and rows in $N$ impose finitely many upper bounds $t\le U_q:=(h_q-a_q\cdot u)/\alpha_q$. Each pair row is exactly $L_p\le U_q$. To justify the finite extrema, enumerate any nonempty finite list $\ell_0,\ldots,\ell_{k-1}$ of lower bounds and define $M_1=\ell_0$; for $1\le j<k$, recursively set $M_{j+1}=M_j$ if $M_j\ge\ell_j$, and $M_{j+1}=\ell_j$ otherwise. Totality of the real order makes $M_{j+1}$ the larger of $M_j$ and $\ell_j$; induction [F4] shows that $M_k$ is a member of the list and dominates every entry. Thus a finite nonempty list has a maximum. For an upper-bound list $v_0,\ldots,v_{\ell-1}$, define $m_1=v_0$ and, for $1\le j<\ell$, set $m_{j+1}=m_j$ if $m_j\le v_j$, and $m_{j+1}=v_j$ otherwise. The same totality and induction argument shows $m_\ell$ is a member no greater than any entry, so every finite nonempty list has a minimum. If both $P,N$ are nonempty, these extrema and all pair inequalities give $\max_{p\in P}L_p\le\min_{q\in N}U_q$, so choose $t=\max_{p\in P}L_p$. If only lower bounds occur choose their finite maximum; if only upper bounds occur choose their finite minimum; if neither occurs choose $t=0$. Thus the original system has a solution. [A1, F1, F2, F3, F4, F5, algebra]
